1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,522
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science A,

2
00:00:05,522 --> 00:00:11,546
Unit 2, part one: topics two point one to
two point six. We'll teach our programs to

3
00:00:11,546 --> 00:00:15,275
make decisions with if statements and
Boolean logic.

4
00:00:16,448 --> 00:00:21,248
So far, our programs run every line, top
to bottom, exactly once.

5
00:00:21,698 --> 00:00:26,711
Real programs make choices, and repeat
work. That's what Unit two is about.

6
00:00:27,861 --> 00:00:30,805
Every algorithm is built from three
building blocks.

7
00:00:31,255 --> 00:00:36,944
Sequencing means doing steps in order.
Selection means choosing a path based on a

8
00:00:36,944 --> 00:00:40,877
condition. Repetition means doing steps
again and again.

9
00:00:42,027 --> 00:00:44,544
Selection answers a yes or no question.

10
00:00:44,994 --> 00:00:48,855
If it's raining, take an umbrella.
Otherwise, wear sunglasses.

11
00:00:49,305 --> 00:00:51,225
Only one of the two paths runs.

12
00:00:52,375 --> 00:00:55,362
Repetition keeps going while a condition
is true.

13
00:00:55,812 --> 00:00:58,585
While the dishes aren't done, wash one
more dish.

14
00:00:59,035 --> 00:01:01,339
Each time through is called an iteration.

15
00:01:02,489 --> 00:01:07,908
Most algorithms mix all three. Here's one
for finding the biggest number in a list.

16
00:01:08,358 --> 00:01:13,252
Set max to the first number. Then, for
each other number, if it's bigger than

17
00:01:13,252 --> 00:01:14,459
max, set max to it.

18
00:01:14,909 --> 00:01:18,088
Sequence, repetition, and selection,
working together.

19
00:01:19,238 --> 00:01:23,932
Before you write code, it helps to write
the steps in plain words, or draw a

20
00:01:23,932 --> 00:01:24,550
flowchart.

21
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,011
Diamonds are decisions. Arrows that loop
back are repetition.

22
00:01:30,161 --> 00:01:35,377
Your turn. Keep asking for a password
until it's correct. Is that selection or

23
00:01:35,377 --> 00:01:36,113
repetition?

24
00:01:36,563 --> 00:01:37,843
Pause the video now.

25
00:01:38,293 --> 00:01:42,111
It's repetition. The asking repeats while
the password is wrong.

26
00:01:42,561 --> 00:01:47,425
Next, we'll build the conditions that make
these choices: Boolean expressions.

27
00:01:48,575 --> 00:01:54,289
Every choice a program makes comes down to
a question with only two answers: true or

28
00:01:54,289 --> 00:01:54,698
false.

29
00:01:55,148 --> 00:01:57,857
In Java, those values have the type
boolean.

30
00:01:59,007 --> 00:02:03,679
A Boolean expression is any expression
that evaluates to true or false.

31
00:02:04,129 --> 00:02:08,524
The most common ones compare two values
with a relational operator.

32
00:02:09,674 --> 00:02:12,170
Java has six relational operators.

33
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:18,181
Less than, greater than, less than or
equal to, greater than or equal to, equal

34
00:02:18,181 --> 00:02:19,660
to, and not equal to.

35
00:02:20,110 --> 00:02:25,294
Notice that equal to is two equals signs.
One equals sign is assignment.

36
00:02:26,444 --> 00:02:29,046
Let's evaluate some. Say x is seven.

37
00:02:29,496 --> 00:02:35,711
x greater than five is true. x equals
equals ten is false. x not equals ten is

38
00:02:35,711 --> 00:02:36,110
true.

39
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:40,741
x percent two equals equals one is true,
because seven is odd.

40
00:02:41,891 --> 00:02:43,854
That last one is a common pattern.

41
00:02:44,304 --> 00:02:49,104
A number percent two equals equals zero is
true when the number is even.

42
00:02:49,554 --> 00:02:55,122
And n percent k equals equals zero tells
you whether n is a multiple of k.

43
00:02:56,272 --> 00:02:58,853
You can store a Boolean result in a
variable.

44
00:02:59,303 --> 00:03:03,143
boolean passed equals score greater than
or equal to sixty.

45
00:03:03,593 --> 00:03:05,854
If score is seventy two, passed is true.

46
00:03:07,004 --> 00:03:12,822
Your turn. int a equals four, int b equals
nine. What is b percent a equals equals

47
00:03:12,822 --> 00:03:13,106
one?

48
00:03:13,556 --> 00:03:14,836
Pause the video now.

49
00:03:15,286 --> 00:03:19,531
Nine percent four is one. One equals one,
so the answer is true.

50
00:03:19,981 --> 00:03:24,333
Next, we'll use Boolean expressions to
make decisions with if statements.

51
00:03:25,483 --> 00:03:30,304
A Boolean expression asks a question. An
if statement acts on the answer.

52
00:03:30,754 --> 00:03:32,696
This is how Java makes a choice.

53
00:03:33,846 --> 00:03:39,064
Here's the shape. The word if, a condition
in parentheses, then a block in curly

54
00:03:39,064 --> 00:03:39,520
braces.

55
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:44,493
If the condition is true, the block runs.
If it's false, Java skips it.

56
00:03:45,643 --> 00:03:47,712
An if else statement gives two paths.

57
00:03:48,162 --> 00:03:51,704
If temp is above thirty, print hot. Else,
print not hot.

58
00:03:52,154 --> 00:03:55,140
Exactly one of the two blocks runs, never
both.

59
00:03:56,290 --> 00:03:58,594
For more than two choices, chain else if.

60
00:03:59,044 --> 00:04:04,228
Java checks each condition from the top,
and runs the first block whose condition

61
00:04:04,228 --> 00:04:06,276
is true. Then it skips the rest.

62
00:04:06,726 --> 00:04:08,838
So the order of the conditions matters.

63
00:04:09,988 --> 00:04:13,210
Let's trace a grade program. Score is
eighty five.

64
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:18,988
Is it at least ninety? No. At least
eighty? Yes. So grade is B, and Java skips

65
00:04:18,988 --> 00:04:20,422
the remaining checks.

66
00:04:21,572 --> 00:04:25,284
A classic mistake: a semicolon right after
the condition.

67
00:04:25,734 --> 00:04:31,379
if, x greater than five, semicolon. That
semicolon ends the if statement, so the

68
00:04:31,379 --> 00:04:33,073
block below always runs.

69
00:04:33,523 --> 00:04:39,289
Another tip: always use curly braces, even
for one line. It prevents bugs when you

70
00:04:39,289 --> 00:04:40,414
add lines later.

71
00:04:41,564 --> 00:04:47,167
Your turn. n is twelve. If n is greater
than twenty, print A. Else if n is greater

72
00:04:47,167 --> 00:04:50,310
than ten, print B. Else, print C. What
prints?

73
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:52,040
Pause the video now.

74
00:04:52,490 --> 00:04:57,333
Twelve isn't greater than twenty, but it
is greater than ten. So it prints B.

75
00:04:57,783 --> 00:05:01,004
Next, we'll put if statements inside other
if statements.

76
00:05:02,155 --> 00:05:05,462
Sometimes a decision depends on an earlier
decision.

77
00:05:05,912 --> 00:05:10,200
That's when you put one if statement
inside another. It's called nesting.

78
00:05:11,350 --> 00:05:16,797
Here's an example. If the user is logged
in, then check: if they're an admin, show

79
00:05:16,797 --> 00:05:19,520
the admin page. Else, show the home page.

80
00:05:19,970 --> 00:05:22,530
If they aren't logged in, show the login
page.

81
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:26,308
The inner if only runs when the outer
condition is true.

82
00:05:27,458 --> 00:05:29,314
Let's trace it with three users.

83
00:05:29,764 --> 00:05:35,968
Logged in and admin: admin page. Logged
in, not admin: home page. Not logged in:

84
00:05:35,968 --> 00:05:39,535
login page, and the inner if is never
checked.

85
00:05:40,685 --> 00:05:42,456
Watch out for the dangling else.

86
00:05:42,906 --> 00:05:47,763
Without braces, an else matches the
closest if above it, no matter how it's

87
00:05:47,763 --> 00:05:48,346
indented.

88
00:05:48,796 --> 00:05:52,977
Indentation is for people. Java only
follows the braces.

89
00:05:54,127 --> 00:05:59,418
Nested ifs can often be flattened into an
else if chain, which is easier to read.

90
00:05:59,868 --> 00:06:04,420
Use nesting when the inner question only
makes sense after the outer one is

91
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:04,966
answered.

92
00:06:06,116 --> 00:06:09,871
On the AP exam, trace nested ifs one level
at a time.

93
00:06:10,321 --> 00:06:14,886
Evaluate the outer condition first. Only
step inside when it's true.

94
00:06:16,036 --> 00:06:22,069
Your turn. x is five and y is two. If x is
greater than three: if y is greater than

95
00:06:22,069 --> 00:06:26,575
three, print A, else print B. Otherwise,
print C. What prints?

96
00:06:27,025 --> 00:06:28,305
Pause the video now.

97
00:06:28,755 --> 00:06:34,628
x is greater than three, so we go inside.
y isn't greater than three, so it prints

98
00:06:34,628 --> 00:06:34,771
B.

99
00:06:35,221 --> 00:06:38,549
Next, we'll combine conditions with and,
or, and not.

100
00:06:39,699 --> 00:06:44,606
A movie ticket is cheaper if you're under
twelve, or if you're sixty five and over.

101
00:06:45,056 --> 00:06:50,752
That's two conditions in one decision.
Java joins them with logical operators.

102
00:06:51,902 --> 00:06:54,376
Java has three logical operators.

103
00:06:54,826 --> 00:06:58,816
And is two ampersands. It's true only when
both sides are true.

104
00:06:59,266 --> 00:07:03,618
Or is two vertical bars. It's true when at
least one side is true.

105
00:07:04,068 --> 00:07:08,612
Not is an exclamation mark. It flips true
to false, and false to true.

106
00:07:09,762 --> 00:07:11,703
A truth table shows every case.

107
00:07:12,153 --> 00:07:17,678
For and, only true and true gives true.
For or, only false or false gives false.

108
00:07:18,828 --> 00:07:24,859
Let's check a range. To test whether x is
between one and ten, write x greater than

109
00:07:24,859 --> 00:07:28,492
or equal to one, and, x less than or equal
to ten.

110
00:07:28,942 --> 00:07:34,894
You can't write one less than or equal to
x less than or equal to ten in Java. Each

111
00:07:34,894 --> 00:07:37,476
comparison needs its own expression.

112
00:07:38,626 --> 00:07:41,122
Java uses short circuit evaluation.

113
00:07:41,572 --> 00:07:46,864
With and, if the left side is false, the
whole thing must be false, so Java skips

114
00:07:46,864 --> 00:07:47,844
the right side.

115
00:07:48,294 --> 00:07:52,112
With or, if the left side is true, Java
skips the right side.

116
00:07:52,562 --> 00:07:58,106
That's useful. In count not equals zero
and total divided by count greater than

117
00:07:58,106 --> 00:08:01,544
five, the division never runs when count
is zero.

118
00:08:02,694 --> 00:08:07,216
When an expression mixes operators, not
goes first, then and, then or.

119
00:08:07,666 --> 00:08:11,037
When in doubt, add parentheses. They make
your meaning clear.

120
00:08:12,187 --> 00:08:16,027
Your turn. a is true and b is false. What
is not a, or b?

121
00:08:16,477 --> 00:08:17,757
Pause the video now.

122
00:08:18,207 --> 00:08:20,660
Not a is false. False or false is false.

123
00:08:21,110 --> 00:08:25,334
Next, we'll see when two different Boolean
expressions mean the same thing.

124
00:08:26,484 --> 00:08:31,420
Two Boolean expressions are equivalent if
they give the same result in every

125
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:32,329
possible case.

126
00:08:32,779 --> 00:08:37,089
The AP exam loves asking whether two
conditions are equivalent.

127
00:08:38,239 --> 00:08:43,489
One way to check is a truth table. List
every combination of inputs, and compare

128
00:08:43,489 --> 00:08:44,276
the results.

129
00:08:44,726 --> 00:08:48,225
If any row is different, the expressions
are not equivalent.

130
00:08:49,375 --> 00:08:53,087
De Morgan's laws give a shortcut for
negating and, and or.

131
00:08:53,537 --> 00:08:56,609
Not, a and b, is the same as not a, or not
b.

132
00:08:57,059 --> 00:09:00,216
Not, a or b, is the same as not a, and not
b.

133
00:09:00,666 --> 00:09:04,250
Push the not inside, and flip and to or,
or or to and.

134
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:10,221
Here's a real example. Not, x greater than
zero and x less than ten.

135
00:09:10,671 --> 00:09:15,194
Apply De Morgan: not x greater than zero,
or not x less than ten.

136
00:09:15,644 --> 00:09:21,881
Then flip each comparison: x less than or
equal to zero, or x greater than or equal

137
00:09:21,881 --> 00:09:22,407
to ten.

138
00:09:23,557 --> 00:09:29,451
Notice how comparisons flip. Not less than
becomes greater than or equal to. Not

139
00:09:29,451 --> 00:09:31,514
equal to becomes not equals.

140
00:09:31,964 --> 00:09:35,185
A common mistake is to forget the or equal
part.

141
00:09:36,335 --> 00:09:39,898
Equivalent expressions are also a tool for
cleaner code.

142
00:09:40,348 --> 00:09:45,084
You can rewrite a confusing negation into
a version that reads naturally.

143
00:09:46,234 --> 00:09:52,519
Your turn. Which is equivalent to not, a
or b? Option one: not a or not b. Option

144
00:09:52,519 --> 00:09:54,149
two: not a and not b.

145
00:09:54,599 --> 00:09:55,879
Pause the video now.

146
00:09:56,329 --> 00:09:59,827
Option two. Not, a or b, equals not a and
not b.

147
00:10:00,277 --> 00:10:04,672
That's the end of selection. Next, we'll
start repetition with while loops.

148
00:10:05,822 --> 00:10:10,499
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

149
00:10:10,499 --> 00:10:11,774
miss the next lesson.

150
00:10:12,224 --> 00:10:13,120
See you there.

